    <section class="source-note" id="source-note"><div><p class="eyebrow">A printed source you can inspect</p><h2>One edition. Two languages.</h2><p>Greek text and Latin translation from J. L. Heiberg’s <cite>Euclidis opera omnia</cite>, volume I (1883), printed pages 2–57. The English and reading notes are new. Point labels stay Greek in all three languages.</p><button type="button" id="open-edition">Open the printed pages</button></div><div class="editorial-note"><h3>What changed in transcription</h3><p>Line breaks and inline reference marks are omitted. Greek ϑ is written θ; point labels use consistent Greek letters. Latin u/v spelling is retained. Square brackets in the definitions, common notions, I.1, I.4, I.8, I.13 and I.15 reproduce Heiberg’s editorial brackets. Angle and triangle symbols are written ∠ and △. The step divisions, geometry orientation, reasons and annotations are teaching aids. The usual five-common-notion numbering is used in the notes; Heiberg’s expanded list is identified where it differs.</p><p>The English sometimes names intersections implied by the Greek. It is a reading translation, not a critical edition. The language notes cover selected forms, not every word.</p><a href="/stoicheia/sources/manifest.json">Source and image provenance</a></div></section>
